// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// specs/t27f/token_floor.t27 -- an infographic in which every particle carries a fact: what one
// token of a chat reply costs in energy, set against the least energy physics allows for it.
//
// WHY: the owner, 2026-10-18, on the engine reel: the particles there fly but carry no information;
// where is the infographic built on this technology? This is it. The numbers come from
// specs/physics/physical_limits.t27, the forces from specs/t27f/particle_engine.t27, and the frame
// helpers from specs/t27f/reel_particles.t27. Nothing here restates any of them.
//
// THE FACT, as physical_limits computes it:
//     the floor   picking one token out of a 128256-token vocabulary names 17 bits; erasing 17 bits
//                 at 300 K costs at least 17 k T ln 2 = 4.9e-20 J (R. Landauer, IBM J. Res. Dev. 5,
//                 183, 1961)
//     the bill    0.24 Wh for a median text prompt (Google, 2025, arXiv:2508.15734), over a reply of
//                 500 tokens: 1.7 J a token
//     the gap     1.7 J / 4.9e-20 J = 3.5e19: 19 whole decades. The bill is engineering, not
//                 thermodynamics.
// The vocabulary size and the reply length are UNVERIFIED illustrative inputs in physical_limits,
// and the picture says so in its footer.
//
// THE PICTURE: a glass tube stands on a glossy black floor. Twenty glass balls drop into it one by
// one. The first, white, is the floor: 4.9e-20 J. Each gold ball after it multiplies by ten, so the
// column is a logarithmic bar chart that builds itself: 19 gold balls, one per decade of the gap.
// The tube holds the balls in line, so their motion is one-dimensional: gravity g = G_STD, Hertz
// contact between neighbours and with the floor (contact_f_over_r) with the engine's dashpot
// C_CONTACT, integrated by velocity Verlet at STEPS substeps of H per frame. Air drag on a 34 mm
// ball at these speeds is under 5 percent of its weight and is left out.
// Every letter is made of particles. Each lit dot of a 5x7 dot-matrix glyph is a body steered to
// its place by the engine's critically damped PD controller (KP, KD): control enters as a force,
// never as a position. The labels of a ball are emitted by that ball at the frame the integrator
// finds it touching the stack: the floor label from the white ball, the decade tick beside each
// ball from the ball itself. The exponent of the counter "X10^k" is one pool of particles that
// re-forms into k each time gold ball k lands: the dots k needs beyond k - 1 leave ball k, the ones
// it no longer needs go out where they stand. A ball lands every 0.3 s, so the counter steers with
// the same controller made five times as fast, KP 25 and KD 5 times over, still critically damped.
// What the light does is derived from the state, as in the engine: a dot and a ball glow by
// glow_of(v); the floor mirrors everything with the Fresnel reflectance of lacquer at the angle
// the engine's camera sees it (mirror_alpha); light adds and the exposure 1 - exp(-L) never clips.
//
// THE OUTPUT: `main` writes FRAMES frames of FRAME_W x FRAME_H raw RGB24 to stderr, row by row, as
// specs/t27f/reel_particles.t27 does; ffmpeg reads them with plain flags
// (`-f rawvideo -pix_fmt rgb24 -s 1080x1920 -r 30 -i frames.rgb`).
//
// NOT CLAIMED: the ball mass, the drop height, the tube and the light levels are design choices,
// labelled as such. The balls are not a model of any computer; they are a bar chart that obeys
// Newton.
// phi^2 + 1/phi^2 = 3 | TRINITY

module token_floor;

